\subsection{Pipeline Architecture}

PurpleTeam Suite implements a ten-stage linear pipeline in which the output of each stage becomes the input of the next. The system is built as an Electron desktop application using React and TypeScript, with all backend logic isolated from the user interface through Electron's IPC bridge. This separation is a deliberate security constraint: the renderer process has no direct access to the file system, network scanning tools or external APIs, and all communication passes through pre-approved channels registered in the main process.
 
Figure \ref{fig:pipeline} illustrates the full pipeline architecture. The system proceeds through three functional groups: discovery, in which the target is validated, scanned across two progressive phases and parsed into structured JSON; analysis, in which findings are classified against the OWASP Top 10 and scored with exploitability-aware weighting; and AI enrichment, in which the Gemini API generates plain-English summaries and remediation steps whose output is then cross-validated by the hallucination guard before report generation. Each stage is implemented as an independent module with its own type definitions and barrel export, which means that any module can be replaced, extended or disabled without affecting adjacent stages. The dashed feedback path in the diagram represents the iterative scan-remediate-rescan lifecycle supported by the delta comparison system described in Section \ref{sec:lifecycle}.
 
