\subsection{Classification and Scoring}

Vulnerability classification uses a dual-layer approach. A deterministic keyword matcher maps each vulnerability to OWASP Top 10 categories using weighted keyword scoring across multiple fields, with regex special-cases for common vulnerability classes and a structured fallback chain for ambiguous findings. This deterministic layer runs before the AI component sees the data, establishing a ground-truth classification that the hallucination guard can later compare against the model's independent assessment.
 
The security scoring model was designed to address the limitations the literature identifies with static CVSS-based prioritisation~\cite{howland2023,jacobs2023}. Rather than treating all findings of the same severity as equally dangerous, the scorer applies four contextual multipliers that stack multiplicatively on each vulnerability's base deduction: port exposure weighting amplifies findings on commonly targeted ports, service exposure amplifies findings affecting database or administrative services, an authentication weakness multiplier increases the cost of findings suggesting credential flaws, and a flat TLS absence penalty applies once when HTTP services are detected without HTTPS. All multipliers are defined as named constants and are fully deterministic, producing identical scores from identical inputs. A runtime feature toggle allows contextual weighting to be disabled entirely for ablation testing, reverting the scorer to flat severity-only deductions. Figure \ref{fig:dashboard} presents the dashboard view of a completed scan, showing the security score, OWASP Top 10 coverage matrix and hallucination metrics alongside the prioritised vulnerability table.

