\subsection{AI Integration and Hallucination Control}

The LLM component sends compressed vulnerability data to Google Gemini with structured output enforcement, low temperature settings and a constrained JSON response schema. Each vulnerability receives a plain-English summary, severity justification, affected endpoints and three remediation steps. The architectural decision to compress and filter scan data before submission ensures the model can only analyse what the scanner actually found, rather than speculating about systems it has not observed, a constraint motivated by the classification reliability concerns raised by Lin et al.~\cite{lin2025} and Chen et al.~\cite{chen2024}.
 
The hallucination guard operates as a post-AI validation layer. It cross-references the model's OWASP category assignments against the deterministic keyword mapper's independent classifications, verifies that any CVE identifiers mentioned in the AI output actually appear in the scan data, checks for disproportionate confidence gaps between the model and the keyword matcher, and flags vulnerabilities where remediation steps are missing. Each finding receives a per-vulnerability risk level, and an overall trust score is computed using a weighted formula that penalises fabricated CVEs most heavily, OWASP disagreements moderately, and confidence mismatches least. These metrics are persisted longitudinally to enable reliability tracking across scans. Figure \ref{fig:ai-detail} illustrates the AI-generated analysis for a single vulnerability, including the plain-English summary, affected endpoints, structured remediation steps and OWASP classification with confidence level.

