Two architectural decisions warrant specific justification. First, the choice to spawn Nmap as an external child process rather than embedding a scanning library was driven by the need to use Nmap's full scripting engine, including the \texttt{vulners} and \texttt{http-headers} NSE scripts, which are not available through wrapper libraries. This design accepts an external dependency in exchange for access to the complete Nmap capability set. Second, the decision to use a single LLM provider (Gemini) with a model fallback chain rather than a multi-provider abstraction layer was a deliberate scope constraint. A 4-model fallback chain (\texttt{gemini-2.5-flash} as primary, with three progressively smaller alternatives) provides resilience against individual model unavailability without introducing the complexity of cross-provider prompt normalisation. The trade-off is vendor dependency, which is acknowledged as a limitation in Section 3.4.

The development and testing environment consisted of a Windows 11 workstation with Nmap installed on the system PATH. The controlled localhost testbed was served through Node.js on port 8080. All scanning was conducted over the local network or against public authorised targets, with no traffic routed through external proxies or VPNs. Build verification was additionally performed on Kali Linux to confirm that the Electron packaging, Nmap child process spawning and file system persistence behaved consistently across platforms.

