\section{Evaluation Framework}
\label{sec:evaluation-framework}

The evaluation is structured across five phases, each producing quantitative data
against defined metrics. All testing is conducted on five authorised targets
that collectively represent a range of technology stacks, deployment models and
vulnerability profiles. Two targets are locally hosted: a controlled Node.js
testbed served on \texttt{127.0.0.1:8080} with 12 intentional vulnerabilities
across six OWASP categories, and an OWASP Mutillidae~II Docker instance running
at Security Level~0 with Apache, PHPMyAdmin, OpenLDAP and HTTPS services
exposed across ports 80, 81, 82, 389 and 443. Three targets are public:
\texttt{scanme.nmap.org} (Nmap Project, explicitly authorised for port scanning),
\texttt{testasp.vulnweb.com} (Acunetix IIS/ASP/MSSQL test site) and
\texttt{demo.testfire.net} (HCL/IBM deliberately vulnerable banking application).
The two locally hosted environments are fully controllable, permitting deliberate
vulnerability configuration and remediation between scan cycles. The three public
targets provide externally maintained intentionally vulnerable environments
against which pipeline behaviour can be verified without modification.

Phase~1 establishes baseline measurements by running the full pipeline against
all five targets with contextual weighting disabled, and repeating each scan to
verify result consistency. Phase~2 enables contextual weighting and repeats the
scans, producing a direct comparison of scores with and without
exploitability-aware adjustment. Phase~3 performs ablation testing, in which
individual pipeline features are disabled one at a time and the same scan data
is reprocessed to isolate the contribution of each component. The features
tested in isolation are the OWASP keyword mapper, the AI analysis component,
the hallucination guard and the contextual weighting layer. A runtime feature
toggle system was implemented to support this methodology, allowing each feature
to be switched off without modifying the codebase or re-scanning.

Phase~4 demonstrates the iterative lifecycle capability. A subset of the
localhost testbed's vulnerabilities are remediated, the target is rescanned,
and a delta comparison report is generated to measure score improvement,
vulnerability resolution and OWASP coverage contraction. The localhost testbed
is used for this phase because its vulnerability configuration is under direct
control, permitting precise remediation and reassessment within a single
session. Phase~5 evaluates hallucination guard reliability by accumulating
trust score, OWASP disagreement rate, confidence mismatch rate and fabricated
CVE count across multiple scans of different targets, producing a longitudinal
dataset for assessing AI output stability.

The evaluation metrics are drawn directly from the proposal's objectives and
the constraints the literature review establishes: classification correctness
measured against known ground-truth vulnerabilities in the controlled testbeds,
remediation quality assessed through the presence and relevance of structured
guidance, OWASP category coverage breadth, and hallucination detection
performance quantified through the trust scoring mechanism. The five phases are summarised in Table~\ref{tab:eval-framework}.

