\section{Phase 1: Baseline Consistency and Reproducibility}
\label{sec:phase1-results}

The severity deduction scores across the five targets range from 50 to 181, with
four of the five targets receiving an F grade and only testasp.vulnweb.com achieving
a D at 50 deduction points, as shown in Table~\ref{tab:baseline-results} and
Figure~\ref{fig:baseline-scores}. Mutillidae produced the highest total vulnerability
count at 26 findings across all severity tiers, which is consistent with its design
as a deliberately extensive multi-service attack surface exposing Apache, PHPMyAdmin,
OpenLDAP and HTTPS endpoints simultaneously. The demo.testfire.net target produced
the second-largest dataset at 18 vulnerabilities including 3 Critical findings, which
is the highest Critical count in the evaluation and reflects the breadth of its
deliberately vulnerable banking application surface. The controlled testbed and
scanme.nmap.org returned 15 and 6 vulnerabilities respectively, while
testasp.vulnweb.com produced the smallest dataset at 5 findings with zero
Informational entries, which suggests that its IIS/ASP/MSSQL stack exposes fewer
network-layer indicators than the Apache/PHP configurations used by the local targets.

The OWASP distribution presented in Table~\ref{tab:baseline-owasp} and
Figure~\ref{fig:owasp-heatmap} confirms that four of the five targets achieved
4/10 category coverage, with detections concentrated in A01 (Broken Access Control),
A02 (Cryptographic Failures), A03 (Injection) and A05 (Security Misconfiguration).
The exception is demo.testfire.net, which achieved 5/10 coverage by additionally
triggering A07 (Identification and Authentication Failures) detections, a category
that the other four targets did not activate. This additional coverage is consistent
with the target's financial-application design, which exposes authentication
endpoints that Nmap's NSE scripts can partially enumerate. The A05 category dominates
across all targets, with Mutillidae recording 12 A05 findings alone, which is a
pattern that reflects the network-layer scanner's strength in detecting service
misconfigurations and exposed administrative interfaces.

