The contextual weighting system produced a 69.2\% amplification in total severity
deductions when applied to the Mutillidae baseline, raising the total from 107
points under flat scoring to 181 points with contextual multipliers active, as
shown in Table~\ref{tab:contextual-comparison} and
Figure~\ref{fig:contextual-comparison}. This amplification demonstrates that
port exposure on web-facing services is the dominant contextual factor within the
current multiplier model, since Mutillidae exposes multiple HTTP and HTTPS
endpoints on standard web ports that the \texttt{contextualWeighting} module
identifies as high-exposure services warranting a 1.5$\times$ multiplier. The
multiplier heatmap in Figure~\ref{fig:multiplier-heatmap} confirms this pattern,
showing that port exposure registers at 1.5$\times$ for web-facing ports
(principally port~80), while the service exposure and authentication weakness
channels both remain at 1.0$\times$ throughout the entire vulnerability set,
which means that these two multiplier dimensions had no measurable effect on the
Mutillidae target.

The observation that only one of the three multiplier channels activated on this
target is a constraint that limits the conclusions that can be drawn about the
full contextual weighting model from this dataset alone. The service exposure and
authentication weakness multipliers are designed to respond to conditions such as
default credentials, unpatched service versions and weak authentication
configurations, none of which triggered on the Mutillidae deployment at Security
Level~0. This finding suggests that the multiplier system would benefit from a
broader range of service types and authentication configurations in future
testing, a point that is revisited in the limitations discussion in
Section~\ref{sec:eval-limitations}.

The combined multiplier of 1.5$\times$ applied to approximately 60\% of the
vulnerability set accounts for the bulk of the 74-point differential between
flat and contextual scoring, confirming that the weighting system meaningfully
differentiates risk profiles beyond flat CVSS-style severity assignment. The
multiplier constants themselves are researcher-defined heuristics rather than
empirically calibrated values, as acknowledged in Section~\ref{sec:system-design},
which is a design decision that prioritises transparency and adjustability over
statistical precision.

