Disabling \texttt{contextualWeighting} produced the largest single-component
delta at $-74$ points, reducing total severity deductions from 181 to 107, which
confirms contextual weighting as the highest-impact component in the scoring
pipeline. This result is consistent with the Phase~2 finding that port exposure
multipliers amplify deductions by 69.2\%, and it demonstrates that the contextual
layer accounts for approximately 41\% of the total severity deduction in the full
pipeline configuration. The magnitude of this delta validates the design decision
to implement exploitability-aware weighting as a distinct, toggleable module rather
than embedding it within the base severity calculation, since operators who prefer
flat scoring can disable the multiplier system without affecting the remainder of
the pipeline, as described in Section~\ref{sec:system-design}.

Disabling \texttt{owaspMapping} reduced total deductions by 15 points, from 181
to 166, because all vulnerabilities collapsed to the A05 (Security
Misconfiguration) fallback category when the deterministic mapper was removed.
This collapse eliminates the category breadth penalty that the scoring engine
applies when vulnerabilities span multiple OWASP categories, which is a penalty
designed to reflect the increased organisational risk of a diverse vulnerability
profile. The relatively modest 15-point reduction confirms that the breadth
penalty contributes a secondary but measurable effect to the overall score, and
it also demonstrates that the fallback mechanism operates as designed by assigning
a conservative default rather than discarding unmapped findings entirely.

Disabling \texttt{aiAnalysis} produced a zero-point delta, which is the expected
result given that the AI component contributes remediation depth and explanatory
quality rather than numerical scoring. The AI module's primary output is the
remediation guidance text and the $+10$ remediation bonus, but this bonus affects
grade interpretation rather than the severity deduction total that the ablation
test measures. Disabling \texttt{hallucinationGuard} reduced deductions by 16
points, from 181 to 165, which indicates that the guard's validation adjustments
contribute a comparable effect to the OWASP mapping breadth penalty. This
16-point contribution reflects the guard's role as a validation layer that applies
confidence-weighted adjustments to AI classifications rather than functioning as
a primary scoring mechanism, which is consistent with the architectural design
described in Section~\ref{sec:system-design}.

