The fabricated CVE count remained at zero across all 11 scans presented in
Table~\ref{tab:hallucination-longitudinal}, which is a strong positive signal
indicating that the AI model did not invent non-existent vulnerability identifiers
in any configuration tested. Trust scores, by contrast, varied considerably across
targets, ranging from 0 on the Mutillidae ``No OWASP'' configuration to
approximately 85 on testasp.vulnweb.com, as visualised in
Figure~\ref{fig:trust-scores}. Only scanme.nmap.org and testasp.vulnweb.com
consistently exceeded the 70-point trust threshold, while the remaining three
targets produced scores between 0 and 55 across all tested configurations.
Figure~\ref{fig:hallucination-breakdown} provides a complementary view of the
risk flag distribution, showing that Mutillidae baseline accumulated the highest
total at approximately 18 flags, while public targets with fewer vulnerabilities
generated approximately 5 to 6 flags each, which is consistent with the expectation
that larger vulnerability sets produce more opportunities for AI classification
disagreements.

The Mutillidae ``No OWASP'' scan dropping to a trust score of 0 is expected
behaviour that confirms the hallucination guard functions as designed. When the
deterministic OWASP mapper is disabled, all vulnerabilities fall back to A05
(Security Misconfiguration), and the guard detects near-total disagreement between
the AI's OWASP classifications and the deterministic assignments, which correctly
drives the trust score to zero. This result demonstrates that the guard is not
simply rubber-stamping AI outputs but is actively comparing AI classifications
against the deterministic reference and penalising configurations where the two
diverge significantly.

The consistently low trust scores on demo.testfire.net (approximately 38 across
both baseline and Phase~2 scans) suggest that the AI model struggled with the
IIS/ASP.NET technology stack that this target presents. The Gemini model used for
AI analysis may have less training exposure to Microsoft web server configurations
compared to the Apache/PHP targets that dominate the local evaluation environment,
which is a constraint that produces higher disagreement rates between AI and
deterministic classification on this target. This pattern, read together with the
high trust scores on the simpler scanme.nmap.org and testasp.vulnweb.com targets,
indicates that trust score variation is at least partially a function of target
complexity and technology stack familiarity rather than a systematic deficiency in
the hallucination guard itself.

