\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

    \subsection{Scoring Determinism}

    Identical inputs produced identical outputs across all repeat scans conducted
    during the evaluation, confirming full deterministic reproducibility of the
    scoring pipeline. The baseline results in Table~\ref{tab:baseline-results}
    were reproduced without variation when the same target configurations were
    rescanned under the same feature toggle settings, which validates the design
    requirement that the deterministic pipeline components (severity calculation,
    OWASP mapping and contextual weighting) produce consistent numerical outputs
    independent of external state. This reproducibility is a prerequisite for the
    ablation and delta analyses conducted in Phases~3 and~4, since any
    non-deterministic variation in the base score would confound the measurement
    of per-component contribution.

    \subsection{Contextual Weighting Validity}

    The 69.2\% amplification observed in Phase~2 confirms that
    exploitability-aware weighting meaningfully differentiates risk profiles
    beyond flat CVSS-style severity assignment, producing a 74-point increase in
    total deductions on the Mutillidae target when port exposure multipliers were
    applied. This amplification demonstrates that the contextual layer adds
    discriminative value to the scoring output, since two targets with identical
    severity distributions but different network exposure profiles would receive
    different scores under the contextual model. The limitation that only port
    exposure activated on the current target set, with service exposure and
    authentication weakness remaining at 1.0$\times$ throughout, suggests that
    the multiplier system would benefit from a broader range of service types and
    authentication configurations in future testing to fully exercise all three
    multiplier channels.

    \subsection{Feature Contribution}

    The ablation results in Table~\ref{tab:ablation-results} establish a clear
    hierarchy of component contribution: contextual weighting is the
    highest-impact component at $-74$ points, followed by the hallucination guard
    at $-16$ points and OWASP mapping at $-15$ points, with AI analysis producing
    a zero-point delta. The AI component's zero-score contribution is by design,
    since its purpose is to generate remediation guidance and explanatory text
    rather than to influence the numerical severity calculation. The
    hallucination guard's 16-point contribution reflects its role as a validation
    layer that applies confidence-weighted adjustments to AI classifications,
    which is a secondary scoring effect rather than a primary one.

    \subsection{Lifecycle Tracking Accuracy}

    The $+57$ point score improvement and 53\% vulnerability reduction observed
    in Phase~4 confirm that the delta comparison system accurately tracks
    remediation progress across consecutive scan cycles on the controlled
    testbed. The system correctly distinguished between the 8 resolved findings
    and the 7 persisting findings without manual intervention, which validates
    the lifecycle tracking capability as described in
    Section~\ref{sec:system-design}. This result demonstrates that the pipeline
    supports the iterative scan-remediate-rescan workflow that the tool is
    designed to facilitate, producing structured delta outputs that an operator
    can use to verify that specific remediation actions achieved their intended
    effect.

    \subsection{AI Reliability}

    Trust scores below the 70 threshold on 3 of the 5 targets indicate that the
    hallucination guard is flagging genuine AI classification inconsistencies
    rather than rubber-stamping outputs, which is a feature of the guard's design
    rather than a deficiency. The guard's purpose is to surface disagreements
    between AI-generated and deterministic OWASP classifications so that
    operators can make informed decisions about which AI outputs to trust, and
    the variation in trust scores across targets demonstrates that this
    disagreement detection mechanism is sensitive to target complexity and
    technology stack characteristics. The zero-fabricated-CVE result across all
    11 scans is a strong positive signal that the AI model is not inventing
    non-existent vulnerability identifiers, which was identified as a primary
    hallucination risk in the literature review
    (Section~\ref{sec:lit-review}).

    \subsection{OWASP Coverage Analysis}
    \label{sec:owasp-coverage-analysis}

    The baseline scans consistently detected vulnerabilities across four of the ten
    OWASP Top~10 categories: A01 (Broken Access Control), A02 (Cryptographic Failures),
    A03 (Injection) and A05 (Security Misconfiguration). The remaining six categories
    received zero detections across four of the five targets. This pattern reflects a
    structural constraint of the scanning layer rather than a deficiency in the
    classification pipeline: the system operates at the network level via Nmap, and the
    four detected categories align with what service enumeration, NSE scripting and
    header analysis can observe. The exception is demo.testfire.net, which achieved 5/10
    coverage by additionally triggering A07 (Identification and Authentication Failures)
    detections, a result consistent with its financial-application design exposing
    authentication endpoints that Nmap's NSE scripts can partially enumerate.

    Categories such as A04 (Insecure Design), A08 (Software and Data Integrity Failures)
    and A09 (Security Logging and Monitoring Failures) are architectural or operational
    concerns that no automated network-layer scanner can detect. A07 (Identification and
    Authentication Failures) requires authenticated session testing in most cases, while
    A10 (Server-Side Request Forgery) requires active input fuzzing against application
    endpoints, both of which sit outside Nmap's capability set as acknowledged in
    Section~\ref{sec:limitations-ethics}.

    The 40\% OWASP coverage figure across four of five targets should be read as the
    system reporting what it can detect rather than inflating coverage beyond its
    scanner's reach. An application-layer integration, for instance coupling OWASP ZAP
    or Nikto as a secondary scanner, would extend coverage into A07 and A10 through
    authenticated crawling and active parameter fuzzing. A combined multi-layer approach
    would broaden coverage but would still leave architectural categories such as A04
    and A09 unaddressed without manual review, since these categories describe
    design-time and operational practices that fall outside the scope of automated
    scanning.

    \subsection{Limitations}
    \label{sec:eval-limitations}

    The evaluation itself surfaced several limitations that extend beyond the
    design-time constraints already acknowledged in
    Section~\ref{sec:limitations-ethics}. Trust scores falling below the 70
    threshold on 3 of the 5 targets suggest that either the threshold requires
    calibration for targets with complex technology stacks, or the AI model's
    prompt engineering requires refinement for non-PHP server environments. The
    demo.testfire.net results in particular indicate that the Gemini model's
    classification accuracy degrades on IIS/ASP.NET targets, which is a
    constraint that would need to be addressed through either model fine-tuning
    or stack-specific prompt templates before the tool could be reliably deployed
    against Microsoft web server infrastructure.

    Additionally, only the port exposure multiplier channel activated across all
    five targets during the evaluation, with the service exposure and
    authentication weakness channels remaining at their 1.0$\times$ baseline
    throughout. This means that the evaluation was unable to measure the
    contribution of two out of three contextual weighting dimensions, which
    limits the conclusions that can be drawn about the full multiplier model's
    behaviour under diverse network conditions. The target set, whilst expanded
    to five, still represents a narrow range of intentionally vulnerable
    applications rather than production environments, which is a constraint that
    limits the generalisability of all quantitative results presented in this
    chapter.

